BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_314920915@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260725T033826Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260725T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260725T150000
SUMMARY:The Lakeshore Psychiatric Hospital and Camp M (Special Extended To
 ur)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nElizabeth Simc
 oe named the area now known as Mimico for the thousands of pigeons that bl
 ackened the sky at the western end of the settlement. Mimico was originall
 y located at the confluence of Mimico Creek and Dundas Street.\n\nWilliam 
 Gamble\, opened a sawmill and built a settlement for the workers nearby. E
 tobicoke's first church\, Christ Church was opened on Church Street which 
 became Royal York Road. Mimico is a walkable neighbourhood and a great pla
 ce to stroll and discover our amazing history.\n\nThe Lakeshore Hospital w
 as built in 1888 as the Mimico Asylum\, its doors opened on January 21\, 1
 889.\n\nThe original idea for the hospital was a series of cottages design
 ed by Doctor Joseph Workman who wanted a resort type feel to the hospital.
  The architect was Kivas Tully\, who worked with gardener Samuel Matheson.
  Most of the buildings were built by the patients themselves\, and helped 
 with laundry duties\, and tended to the vast gardens.\n\nBut a different s
 tory emerged that most Canadians had no idea about. And that was the story
  of Camp M - Mimico.\n\nIn 1887\, the Victoria Industrial School for Boys 
 opened on old Horner Avenue in Mimico\, just blocks from the site of the n
 ew Mimico Hospital for the Insane (Humber College.) The school\, which was
  a juvenile reformatory\, hoped to break the bad boys through moral and ac
 ademic education and old fashioned hard work. I hope there was a positive 
 outcome.\n\nIn 1927\, the school changed its name to the Mimico Reform Sch
 ool. But it was the war years 1939-1945 that really surprised me as a Toro
 ntonian. It was known as Camp M or Internment Camp 22\, the largest camp f
 or POWs in the centre of a large Canadian city.\n\nMost camps were constru
 cted in rural areas\, and the camp provided space for more than 520 German
  nationals. The history of this camp is fascinating and had links to the T
 oronto Central Prison on Strachan Avenue and the Mimico Hospital directly 
 to the south on lakeshore where we begin this tour.\n\nThis walking tour i
 s for all levels and walkers. The tour begins at the New Toronto Public Li
 brary Branch at 110 Eleventh Street just south of Lakeshore. We end this t
 our at Kipling and Horner Avenue\, just a few blocks to the north of the o
 ld hospital!\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person!\n\n\\*\
 \* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your sp
 ace for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**toro
 ntohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/314920
 915/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260523T103759Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260523T103759Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_314948604@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260725T033826Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260726T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260726T160000
SUMMARY:Parkdale (Anniversary Walk - 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**[torontohistorywalks@gmail.com](http://torontohistorywalks@gm
 ail.com/)**\n**for more information or for signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\* **Pl
 ease note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for
  this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**[torontohis
 torywalks@gmail.com](http://torontohistorywalks@gmail.com/)**\n\n**This is
  the 7th anniversary of Toronto History Walks. It was exactly seven years 
 ago on July 27\, 2019 that the first tour of Parkdale ran. I was a very di
 fferent guide then. I can't say that it was the best. I have learned so mu
 ch about this amazing city\, and its amazing past. I learned how to guide\
 , that was the true education and that took a while. I hope all of you hav
 e enjoyed the tours and how the tours are run. Hope to see you for the sev
 enth birthday party\, well maybe not a party but let's celebrate Toronto a
 nyway. I am just getting started in this history thing let me add. Many mo
 re tours to come!**\n\nParkdale has many faces.\n\nAnd over the years thos
 e faces have become sharper and clearer to residents and visitors alike. T
 he tone has changed in Parkdale. And that is always a good thing.\n\nOne o
 f the main reasons why Toronto History Walks came into being was simply be
 cause the images of Parkdale at the time\, 2019\, seemed to convey a negat
 ive picture of the neighbourhood. Toronto residents wanted to avoid Parkda
 le at all costs based on assumptions. It was dangerous and better to avoid
  than explore.\n\nBut those ideas have changed over the last several years
 . Parkdale is a great place to explore\, have a cup of coffee or dinner an
 d check out the arts and entertainment or take a stroll down a side street
  in one of the best preserved Victorian neighbourhoods in Toronto.\n\nWelc
 ome to Toronto's first suburb\, the floral suburb. And it truly is a remar
 kable place to get to know. What Parkdale needed most was understanding. A
 nd this walk provides the interested walker with that understanding of how
  Parkdale came to be\, and why it had a rather sketchy history right from 
 its inception in 1879. And it had to do more with the attitudes of the res
 idents than reality. Attitudes that followed the growth of Parkdale to thi
 s very day. Parkdale just can't get free of its own legacy!\n\nFrom its be
 ginnings in 1850 to the controversy surrounding the Gardiner Expressway\, 
 and the death of Sunnyside Amusement Park\, the story of Parkdale unfolds\
 , and it has a remarkable ability to climb out of depressed economic perio
 ds. This is a detailed exploration of the district.\n\nThis tour begins at
  the southwest corner of King\, Queen\, and Roncesvalles\, at the Polish m
 emorial Katyn\, just east of the Sunnyside pedestrian bridge across the st
 reet from the McDonald's.\n\nThis 2 hour walking tour is for all walking l
 evels. Please dress for the season\, and hope to see you there.\n\nThis to
 ur has a ticket price of $15.00 per person!\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Pleas
 e e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! You 
 can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.c
 om**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/314948
 604/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260525T145210Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260525T145210Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315410755@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260725T033826Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260729T111000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260729T141000
SUMMARY:Summer on the Historic Toronto Islands! 
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the f
 ollowing email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nFor summertime 
 fun\, it's hard to imagine a better place than the Toronto Islands. It's s
 o much more than a small amusement park\, Chelsea Beach\, and walking and 
 bike trails. It's about growing up in Toronto. About the ten minute ride o
 ver on the ferry boat\, and the memories that have made Toronto a summerti
 me city. It's a stretch of land that is historic and fascinating\, both ge
 ologically and socially as well. And a history that brings together the st
 ories of Toronto from its founding.\n\nThe Gibraltar Point Lighthouse was 
 constructed in 1809\, and is one of the oldest structures in Toronto. It i
 s best known for the ghost story of its murdered first keeper\, John Paul 
 Rademüller. But that's another story for another day.\n\nThe peninsula wa
 s partially severed by a storm in 1852\, which created a sandbar that was 
 dangerously thin\, and the peninsula became an island permanently by a vio
 lent storm that cut out the eastern channel\, two hotels on the island wer
 e also destroyed. The modern history of the Islands' begins here!\n\nThe I
 slands' have three main parts\, and all three have their historic elements
 \, the most being the western most island named for the Hanlan family. A s
 pectacular hotel\, "The Ned Hanlan" was built and a regatta course dredged
  out of the western lagoon. An amusement park followed\, and an airport af
 ter that!\n\nIt was also a residential enclave with most of the homes appe
 aring on Ward's and Centre Islands. By the 1950s these residential areas s
 tarted to disappear\, as the idea of increasing city parkland took shape.\
 n\n**Take a quick peek at my introduction video which follows at** https:/
 /youtu.be/hkVHx5Ol9qI\n\nThe tour begins and ends at the Jack Layton Ferry
  Terminal at the foot of Bay Street. We will meet **inside** the gates! An
 d we will head over as a group\, so tickets must be bought before! Please 
 meet 10 minutes before the meetup time!\n\nThis tour has a ticket price of
  $15.00 per person!\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket 
 price when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the fol
 lowing email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315410
 755/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260625T121931Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260625T121931Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_315381066@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260725T033826Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T113000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T133000
SUMMARY:Mount Pleasant Cemetery (WEEKDAY)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n\\*\\***The Toronto History Walks Yearl
 y Pass is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\
 , email**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or fo
 r signing up!\\*\\***\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticke
 t amount when booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the 
 following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nThe only autho
 rized cemeteries within York were Anglican or Roman Catholic. Citizens who
  did not belong to either church had no choice but to find burial arrangem
 ents outside York.\n\nThe General Burying Ground received Royal Assent in 
 1826 and land that came to be known as Potters Field was acquired and star
 ted operation soon afterwards. Over time\, additional cemetery lands were 
 added and in 1873\, a new cemetery available to all citizens was establish
 ed. The new cemetery was situated on the farm land of the Cawthra family n
 ear Yonge Street.\n\nThis walk is for all fitness levels. Please dress for
  the weather. This walk will begin and end outside the Yorkminster Park Ba
 ptist Church at 1585 Yonge Street just north of Heath Street.\n\nThis tour
  has a ticket price of $15.00 per person! This tour has space for **11** e
 xplorers\, so if you do plan on attending please secure your tickets early
 .\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when bookin
 g your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:*
 *\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315381
 066/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260623T100648Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260623T100648Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Meetup: History of Parkdale
UID:event_314948270@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260725T033826Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260731T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260731T130000
SUMMARY:Landmarks 2: Tecumseh Wigwam to the 1977 Reference Library (NEW)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Yearly Pass
  is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, emai
 l**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for sign
 ing up!**\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount whe
 n booking your space for this tour! You can e-transfer to the following em
 ail at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n\nIn this new volume\, Landm
 arks revisits the site of some of Toronto's urban legends\, and historic b
 uildings that gave rise to the city. This walk's focus is Bloor Street Wes
 t\, and Avenue Road\, starting with the rough and tumble saloon known as t
 he Tecumseh Wigwam a boisterous drinking spot at the corner of Avenue Road
  and Bloor now the elegant Park Plaza Hotel.\n\nFrom there we wander aroun
 d the area taking in landmarks not otherwise explored on other Toronto His
 tory Walks tours. So get your brains and feet ready for a workout my frien
 ds.\n\nToronto is a city that hides its history in plain sight. Beneath th
 e glass towers are layers of stories — grand hotels that vanished overni
 ght\, political scandals whispered through stone corridors\, theatres that
  once glittered under gaslight\, and laneways where the city first learned
  how to become a metropolis.\n\nThis new Toronto History Walks series of 6
 0 different walking tours invites the walker to slow down\, and see the ci
 ty differently.\n\nOver the course of 30 carefully chosen stops\, this wal
 king tour traces the landmarks that helped shape the history of Toronto. B
 eginning at the site of the legendary Holland House — once one of the ci
 ty’s finest mansions\, and a gathering place for politicians\, and busin
 essmen\, the route winds through the old heart of downtown\, uncovering st
 ories both famous and forgotten.\n\nSome locations are grand and imposing.
  Others are easy to walk past without a second glance. Every stop reveals 
 a piece of Toronto’s personality. At Osgoode Hall\, visitors will stand 
 before one of the city’s most elegant historic buildings. Nearby\, the o
 ld banks and newspaper buildings of the financial district recall an era w
 hen Toronto proudly called itself “Toronto the Good\,” even as corrupt
 ion and backroom dealings simmered beneath the surface.\n\nThe tour also e
 xplores the vanished city — the Toronto that survives only in photograph
 s\, and memory. Walkers will discover where magnificent Victorian structur
 es once stood before demolition crews erased entire blocks in the name of 
 progress. Former theatres\, hotels\, markets\, and department stores emerg
 e again through storytelling\, helping modern explorers imagine the bustle
 \, smoke\, and noise of earlier generations.\n\nStories of fire\, disaster
 \, celebration\, and reinvention. Participants will hear how the Great Fir
 e of 1904 transformed downtown streets\, how immigrant communities reshape
 d neighbourhoods block by block\, and how Toronto repeatedly reinvented it
 self through booms\, depressions\, wars\, and waves of newcomers.\n\nBut t
 his tour is not simply about dates and buildings. It is about people. The 
 politicians who argued over Confederation. The hotel clerks who witnessed 
 scandals. The newspaper editors who shaped public opinion. The shopkeepers
 \, labourers\, entertainers\, and ordinary Torontonians whose lives unfold
 ed along these same streets.\n\nToronto has always had hidden corners and 
 strange tales: tunnels beneath streets\, forgotten cemeteries beneath offi
 ce towers\, unsolved crimes\, and buildings said to carry echoes of the pa
 st. Even longtime residents may find themselves surprised by what has been
  quietly standing before them all along. Come along and experience a very 
 different city. A deep dive into our history.\n\nThis walking tour begins 
 at the northeast **corner** of Avenue Road\, and Bloor Street West adjacen
 t to the church steps. We end at the Reference Library at Yonge and Asquit
 h just north of Bloor Street East.\n\nThis walking tour is for all walking
  levels. Please dress for the season\, and hope to see you there.\n\nThis 
 tour has a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\n\n\\*\\* **Please note: Ple
 ase e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space for this tour! Yo
 u can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail
 .com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/314948
 270/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260525T143228Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260525T143228Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
